---
import GuideLayout from "@layouts/GuideLayout.astro";

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: "Home", url: "/" },
  { name: "Editorial Policy", url: "/editorial-policy/" }
];
---

<GuideLayout
  title="About and Editorial Policy"
  description="How Lawsuit Status Guide writes, reviews, sources, updates, and keeps public legal information independent."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
  <div class="prose-legal max-w-3xl">
    <h1>About and Editorial Policy</h1>
    <p>Lawsuit Status Guide is a public legal information site for people researching active mass tort and injury lawsuits. It is not a law firm and does not provide legal advice.</p>
    <h2>Editorial responsibility</h2>
    <p><strong>Lawsuit Status Guide Editorial Team</strong> is the byline for the site's guides. The team researches, writes, and maintains them. This byline does not mean that an attorney or medical professional reviewed a page.</p>
    <h2>Sources and review</h2>
    <p>Guides explain allegations, court status, possible records, and deadline considerations using public sources. Case updates link to the court order, JPML report, agency notice, or official settlement record used where available. Editorial checks focus on source support, clear dates, neutral language, and avoiding promises about eligibility, payment, or legal outcomes.</p>
    <h2>What the dates mean</h2>
    <p>A guide's last reviewed or verified date tells readers when its information was checked. A pending-case count has its own source date, such as the date of a JPML report. A later page verification date does not mean the JPML published a new count on that date.</p>
    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>To report an error, outdated detail, or broken source link, email <a href="mailto:contact@lawsuitstatusguide.com">contact@lawsuitstatusguide.com</a>. Include the page URL, the detail in question, and a supporting source if you have one. Please do not send confidential legal or medical records.</p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
